  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonably warm with increasing humidity today. \nSunshine this morning gives way to increasing clouds this afternoon \nahead of an approaching system.\n\nHigh pressure begins to shift offshore today as a developing low \npressure system moves across Quebec and another area of low pressure \ndevelops across the Mid-Atlantic. Warm air advection aloft will \nincrease cloud cover through this afternoon, while temperatures \nremain seasonable, ranging from the upper 70s to lower 80s. Humidity \ngradually increases today, with dew points returning to the lower \n60s and becoming more noticeable compared to Saturday. Most, if not \nall, of the daylight hours remain dry, with showers arriving late \nthis evening into the overnight hours.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers develop late tonight into Monday, though \ncoverage and rainfall amounts have trended lower from previous \nforecasts.\n\nOver the past several nights, we have been monitoring the potential \nfor a more widespread rainfall event late tonight into Monday. \nHowever, the trend continues toward less rainfall, particularly \nacross southern New England. The 00Z guidance generally depicts the \nstrongest forcing associated with a developing weak surface low \nacross the Mid-Atlantic and another area of low pressure near \nnorthern New England and southern Quebec. With southern New England \npositioned between these areas of stronger forcing, it may be \ndifficult to fully capitalize on the higher PWATs of 1.8 to 2.0 \ninches across the region.\n\nNevertheless, periodic showers will develop late tonight and \ncontinue into Monday. There remains high confidence that most of \ntoday remains dry. The majority of the 00Z CAMs keep southern New \nEngland dry through roughly 8 PM, though a few solutions bring \nshowers into western portions of the region earlier. The most \nfavorable period for numerous light to moderate showers appears to \nbe after sunset through roughly 4 to 6 AM Monday.\n\nThe threat for heavy rainfall has also decreased. While an isolated \nheavier downpour cannot be ruled out, widespread heavy rainfall is \nno longer expected. Forecast rainfall totals have generally trended \nto less than a half inch across Connecticut, Rhode Island, and \nsoutheast Massachusetts, with amounts closer to a quarter inch or \nless possible farther north across Massachusetts. Given this trend, \nthe Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall no longer includes southern \nNew England.\n\nShowers should become more scattered during the day Monday, \npotentially resulting in a drier, though cloudy and humid, \nafternoon. A cold front then pushes through the region late Monday \nand may initiate an isolated thunderstorm. However, extensive cloud \ncover and limited instability should limit thunderstorm development \nand intensity. Given the expected cloud cover, showers, and easterly \nflow, Monday will be cooler, with highs ranging from the lower to \nmid-70s across eastern Massachusetts and Rhode Island to around 80 \ndegrees towards the Connecticut River Valley. Dew points remain \nelevated, resulting in rather sticky conditions.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Seasonably warm temperatures and moderate humidity \nreturn through the middle of next week, followed by increasing \nshower chances late in the week.\n\nDrier air advects in from the northwest late Monday night into \nTuesday, leading to another dry stretch Tuesday through Wednesday, \nand possibly into the first half of Thursday, as surface high \npressure builds back into the region. Temperatures remain \nseasonable, with highs in the lower to middle 80s and tolerable \nhumidity as dew points remain in the lower to middle 60s. Ensemble \nguidance continues to signal a transition toward more unsettled \nweather late in the week, with shower chances returning and \ntemperatures potentially trending slightly below normal.",
